course: books
lesson: 49
title: The Wind in the Willows, chapter VII, part 1: The Piper at the Gates of Dawn (Portly is missing)
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: On a hot midsummer night, well past ten o'clock, the Mole lies on cool dock-leaves by the river and waits for the Rat, who has been having supper with the Otters.
explain: The Rat comes back quiet and worried: little Portly, Otter's young son, has been missing for days, and he cannot swim well yet, so everyone is secretly thinking of the weir and of traps.
explain: Otter is too proud to show his fear, but every night he sits by the old ford, where he taught Portly to swim and to fish, just in case the little one wanders back to the place he loves.
explain: The Mole cannot bear to go to bed and do nothing, so the two friends get the boat out and row upstream in the dark, through a night full of small rustles, gurgles and calls.
explain: Then the moon rises and the meadows, gardens and river appear again in silver, like old friends in new clothes; the two search hedges, hollow trees and ditches until the moon sinks and the mystery comes back.
fact: The chapter is called «The Piper at the Gates of Dawn»: a piper is someone who plays a pipe, and «the gates of dawn» means the very beginning of the morning.
fact: It is midsummer, so the night is short and the sky still holds light «though it was past ten o'clock at night».
fact: The Mole had spent the day on the river with some companions; the Rat had «a engagement of long standing» (a promise made long ago) to spend the evening with Otter.
fact: Portly is Otter's little son; he often strays and turns up again, because, says the Mole, «he's so adventurous».
fact: This time it is serious: he has been missing «for some days now», the Otters have hunted everywhere, and «young Portly hasn't learnt to swim very well yet».
fact: A weir is a low wall built across a river; the water pours over it fast and foaming, which is dangerous for a small swimmer.
fact: A ford is a shallow place where you can walk across a river; the old ford was used «in by-gone days before they built the bridge».
fact: At the ford Otter gave Portly his first swimming-lesson and taught him fishing, and there Portly caught his first fish, «of which he was so very proud».
fact: Otter watches there every night «on the chance, you know, just on the chance!» — that is, just in case Portly comes back.
fact: When Otter walked out with the Rat, he said he wanted some air and to stretch his legs — but the Rat could see he was really very nervous.
fact: «I drew him out and pumped him» means the Rat asked Otter gentle questions until he told the whole worry; «turn in» means go to bed.
fact: The Mole says: «I simply can't go and turn in, and go to sleep, and do nothing»; the Rat answers «Just what I was thinking myself»: it's no sort of night for bed anyhow, daybreak is not far off, and they may pick up news of Portly from early risers.
fact: The Rat rows and the Mole steers, carefully, because the shadows on the water look «as solid to all appearance as the banks themselves».
fact: In the dark they keep starting at what seems to be a sudden clear call from a real voice.
fact: When the moon rises, the old places seem to have «put on this pure new apparel» (apparel and raiment are old words for clothes) and wait «to see if they would be recognised».
fact: They search «the hedges, the hollow trees, the runnels and their little culverts»: runnels are tiny streams, culverts are little tunnels carrying water under a path.
fact: Old words: «selvedge» = edge; «torrid» = burning hot; «keeping it up late» = staying up late having fun; «quest» = a search.
fact: Talk in this lesson: «bruv» (London) = mate; «lowkey» = a bit, quietly; «wee» (Scotland) = little; «fixin' to» (southern US) = about to; «heaps» (Australia, NZ) = lots.
exercise: What time is it when the chapter begins? | Past ten o'clock at night | Just after dawn, about five | Midday, in the hottest hour | Eight in the evening, at sunset
exercise: Why is the sky still light so late? | It is midsummer, when the nights are short | There is a great fire burning on the far bank | The full moon is already high in the sky | Toad Hall has every one of its lamps lit
exercise: Where does the Mole lie while he waits for the Rat? | On some cool dock-leaves on the bank | In his own bunk at Mole End, asleep | In the boat, tied up to a willow | On the sofa in the Rat's warm parlour
exercise: Where has the Rat been all evening? | At the Otters', where he had to stay to supper | At Toad Hall, guarding Toad's locked bedroom | At Badger's house in the middle of the Wild Wood | In the village, buying food for the next day
exercise: Who is Portly? | Otter's little son | Badger's young nephew from the Wood | One of the carol-singing field-mice | The elder brother of Billy the hedgehog
exercise: Why does the Mole first think there is no need to worry? | Portly often strays off and always turns up again | Portly is a strong swimmer and can't come to harm | Everyone knows that Portly is staying with Badger | Portly has gone to Town by the early train
exercise: Why is it more serious this time? | He has been gone for days and nobody has seen him | He left a note saying he was off to sea for good | He was seen driving off at speed in Toad's brand-new red motor-car | He was last seen in the Wild Wood among weasels
exercise: What did Otter let slip about Portly's swimming? | He hasn't learnt to swim very well yet | He swims faster than his own father now | He has never once been in the water | He will only swim in the pond at Mole End
exercise: Which two dangers are the friends quietly thinking of? | The weir, and traps | The police, and motor-cars | The snow, and the Wild Wood | The swans, and the swallows
exercise: What is a weir? | A low wall across a river that the water pours over | A small flat-bottomed boat that the otters use for fishing at night | A shallow place where you can walk across a river | A little tunnel that takes a stream under a road
exercise: What is a ford? | A shallow place where you can cross a river on foot | A tall stone bridge with three round arches built high over a river | A deep dark pool where the big fish hide | A wooden gate that holds back the flood water
exercise: Why does Otter watch by the old ford? | He taught Portly to swim and fish there, and hopes he'll come back | It is the only place where otters may sleep in summer | Toad's motor-car once crashed off the bridge into the water just there | The Rat asked him to guard the new bridge from weasels
exercise: Where did Portly catch his first fish? | At the old ford | Just below the weir | At Toad's boat-house | In the gold-fish pond at Mole End
exercise: «I drew him out and pumped him.» What did the Rat do? | Asked Otter questions until he told the truth | Pulled Otter out of the river on a rope | Pumped all the river water out of Otter's leaking little boat | Gave Otter a cold drink from the pump
exercise: How did Otter hide his worry when he walked out with the Rat? | He said he wanted some air and to stretch his legs | He laughed and told a long, funny story about Toad | He said he was off fishing with Portly's cousins | He said he was tired and went straight off to bed
exercise: Who first says that they must go and search, and not go to bed? | The Mole | The Rat | Otter | Badger
exercise: Why does the Rat agree that this is no night for bed? | It is hot, dawn is not far off, and early risers may have news | The moon is far too bright for anyone to sleep tonight | The Mole snores so loudly that nobody can sleep at home | Badger has written a letter asking them both to search all night long, by lantern
exercise: Who rows and who steers when they set out? | The Rat takes the sculls; the Mole steers | The Mole rows; the Rat steers | Both of them pull an oar each and nobody steers at all, since the river is so wide | Otter tows them; they both steer
exercise: Why must the Mole steer so carefully? | Shadows on the water look as solid as the banks | The river is full of sharp rocks and logs | Toad's new boat is moored right across the stream | The weir is pulling every boat towards it
exercise: In the dark the friends keep starting at a noise. What does it seem to be? | A sudden clear call from a real voice | The poop-poop of a motor-car far away | The bells of the village church ringing | The shrill whistling of the weasels
exercise: When the moon rises, what do the old places look like? | Friends in new clothes, waiting shyly to be recognised | Strangers who have moved into the meadows overnight and do not know them at all | The ruins of the old city that Badger told about | A painting of a river hung on Toad's wall
exercise: Where do they search after landing? | Hedges, hollow trees, little streams and ditches | Only the middle of the river, from the boat | The village shops and the railway station | The cellars, the stables and the boat-house of Toad Hall, with a lantern
exercise: What happens when the moon sinks? | Mystery once more holds field and river | The sun rises at once in a blaze | They find Portly asleep on the bank, curled up under a dock-leaf | Otter calls out to them to come home
exercise: Looking back: in chapter I, who vanished in the middle of telling a story about Toad? | The Otter, diving after a May-fly | The Badger, saying «H'm! Company» | The Rat, falling backwards in the boat | The Mole, running home to his whitewash
exercise: Looking back: when the Mole went into the Wild Wood alone (chapter III), how did the Rat know where he had gone? | From the prints of his new goloshes in the mud | From the field-mice, who saw him pass the hedge | From a note left on the parlour table | From the Otter, who met him on the bank
exercise: Looking back: who came through the snowy Wild Wood to find the Rat and the Mole at Badger's? | The Otter, who said people in a fix go to Badger | Toad, driving his brand-new red motor-car | Portly, who was looking for his own father | A rabbit, sent along by the kindly weasels
exercise: ★★★ Otter goes to the ford «every night» and watches «on the chance». Which feeling does the book show without naming it? | Hope mixed with fear | Anger at Portly | Pride in the new bridge | Boredom with fishing
test: Jaz: «Portly's always wandering off, bruv, nothing to stress about this time either.» (bruv = mate) Put it right. | This time he's been gone for days and nobody has seen him | Nothing's wrong; the Rat agrees and goes off to bed | Portly has never once wandered off before tonight | Portly is at home all along and was only hiding from his dad under the bed
test: «Otter's not bothered, lowkey he just fancied a walk.» (lowkey = kind of) Is that right? | No: he talked of air, but he was really very nervous | Yes: Otter never worries about his children | Yes: he was going to buy a new boat in town | No: he went to bed early and slept very well
test: What does the Mole's choice to search tonight show about him now? | He cannot rest while a friend is in trouble | He is bored and wants a ride in the dark | He is scared of sleeping alone in the house | He wants to prove he rows better than the Rat
test: Which is TRUE so far? | The Mole is the one who says they must go and search | The Rat stayed away from the Otters' house all evening | Portly is a strong swimmer who never gets lost | The friends search only by bright daylight
test: Kirsty from Glasgow: «the wee otter's dad sits on the bridge every night.» (wee = little) Put it right. | He watches by the old ford, used before the bridge was built | He watches the weir from the roof of the mill-house | He watches the front gate of Toad Hall, in case Portly went there to play | He watches the dark edge of the Wild Wood
test: Why do the meadows look «washed clean of mystery and terror» when the moon rises? | In the light they can see shapes again, so nothing seems to hide | The rain has washed all the fields and gardens clean | The weasels have run away from the moonlight | The white mist over the river has turned into fresh snow, like the night in the Wild Wood
test: Tyler from Alabama: «they were fixin' to sleep but the Rat said nah, get the boat out.» (fixin' to = about to) Put it right. | It was the Mole who said they should get the boat out | It was Otter who came and ordered them to row | They never went out at all; they slept till morning | Badger knocked at the door and sent them out
test: Mia from Perth: «they looked heaps of places, hedges and trees and ditches, yeah?» (heaps = lots) Is she right? | Yes: hedges, hollow trees, runnels and ditches | No: they only looked down into the water | No: they only searched the village streets | No: they rowed straight to an island and stopped
test: Looking back: in chapter I, what did the Rat say about his river? | «It's my world, and I don't want any other» | «It's a dull fusty old river, and I'm sick of it» | «It's no good for anything but the washing» | «The Wide World is much better than any river»
test: Looking back: in chapter V the Rat turned back on a cold night to find Mole's old home. How is tonight like that? | Again a friend's trouble matters more than their own bed and supper | Again they stop in a village to peep through windows | Again the field-mice come and sing them a carol | Again they lose their way in deep snow
test: Looking back: in chapter I the Mole tipped the boat over. What can he do on the river by now? | Row and steer well, as the Rat taught him | Nothing; he still cannot swim or row | Only fish with a rod from the bank | Only sit in the stern and watch
test: Looking back: the Mole once lay in a hollow beech tree in the Wild Wood. How is tonight different? | Then he hid alone in terror; now he goes out at night on purpose to help | Then he was searching for Portly; now he is looking for Badger | Then he had the Rat with him; now he is all on his own | Then it was a hot summer night; now it is snowy winter
test: Looking back: where was Toad when we last saw him, at the end of chapter VI? | Locked in the grimmest dungeon of an old castle | Asleep in his bunk in the yellow caravan | Crying in Badger's smoking-room | Eating a big lunch at the Red Lion inn
test: ★★★ Careful: who has been missing «for some days», and who sits by the ford «every night»? | Portly is missing; Otter watches | Otter is missing; Portly watches | The Mole is missing; the Rat watches | Portly is missing; the Rat watches
test: ★★★ The ford was used «in by-gone days before they built the bridge». What can we work out? | The bridge is newer, so people cross by it now | The ford is newer than the bridge | The bridge fell down and the ford took its place | Nobody has ever crossed the river at the ford
test: ★★★ It is a little past ten at night. If dawn comes at about four, roughly how many hours of night are left for the search? | About six | About two | About ten | About fourteen
exam: Identify the circumstance which occasioned the anxiety of the Otter family. | The absence for some days of the young otter, an imperfect swimmer | The theft of a valuable motor-car from the precincts of the river | The arrival of an early winter, attended by heavy falls of snow | The disappearance of the Water Rat's boat from its moorings
exam: Ascertain the location at which the elder Otter maintained his nightly watch, together with the reason therefor. | The old ford, where the young one had received his first lessons in swimming | The great weir, whence the young one had last been seen departing | The bridge, being the sole crossing of the river remaining in use | The Wild Wood, wherein his son was believed to be held by weasels
exam: Select the response which most accurately characterises the Mole's resolution upon hearing these tidings. | That a search by boat was preferable to retiring and doing nothing | That the matter was of no consequence, the young one being habitually lost | That the Badger ought first to be consulted in respect of the matter | That any search ought to be deferred until the hour of full daylight
exam: Which of the following statements is consistent with the established facts concerning the rising of the moon? | Familiar places were disclosed anew, as though attired in fresh apparel | The missing child was immediately discovered upon the bank | The moon remained obscured by cloud for the whole of the night | The friends were thereby induced to abandon their search
